{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his young age a regency was established with his mother Hodierna and later his uncle Joscelin II acting as regents. His early years were marked by political instability and constant threats from Muslim forces in the region. The County of Tripoli was one of the four major Crusader states and Raymond III inherited a territory that was vulnerable and in need of strong leadership.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III spent much of his reign navigating complex alliances and rivalries within the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and often served as regent during the reigns of Baldwin IV and Baldwin V. His relationship with Baldwin IV was particularly significant. Despite Baldwin IV suffering from leprosy Raymond III was trusted to manage state affairs and even acted as guardian to the young Baldwin V. Raymond was known for his diplomatic skills and often tried to mediate between the powerful nobles of the kingdom. He was also involved in several military campaigns against Muslim leaders such as Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. His cautious approach to warfare sometimes drew criticism from more aggressive knights but his strategy aimed to preserve the fragile Crusader territories.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III faced personal and political challenges throughout his life. He was captured by Muslim forces in 1164 and held prisoner for several years. His release was secured through a treaty and a large ransom. After his return he continued to play a central role in Crusader politics. He was also accused of involvement in the assassination of his cousin and rival Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly after the disastrous Battle of Hattin. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a capable administrator and a stabilizing force during a turbulent period in the history of the Levant. His life reflected the precarious balance of power among Christian and Muslim forces in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.731181277544238e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.014164154417812824}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.0251946150674485e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.006082831881940365}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.009233392775058746}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.06323573738336563}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0371154530730564e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0005248599336482584}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00033063191222026944}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.2411095415009186e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.2519417405128479}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.004226086661219597}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.8609820219571702e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-